From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elias Bakken <elias@iagent.no>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:33:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] usb: storage: restore USB_READY after a successful transfer

USB_READY means "the device answered TEST UNIT READY, so skip the 5ms
wake-up delay in usb_stor_BBB_transport()". Three error paths clear it,
but the only place that sets it is usb_test_unit_ready(), which runs at
probe. After the first transfer error it therefore stays clear for the
rest of the boot and every later command pays 5ms.

Measured on an A8 loading an 84MB uInitrd off USB: 714 chunks at the
stock 120KB transfer size, so 714 * 5ms = 3.6s of pure delay, taking
throughput from 38.2MB/s down to 14.6MB/s - and it never recovers.

A completed transfer is proof the device is awake, so set the flag again
there. Symmetric with the clear, and it restores full speed immediately
after a transient error.

Still present in u-boot master as of 2026-09; not a Recore-specific bug.

Signed-off-by: Elias Bakken <elias@iagent.no>
---
--- a/common/usb_storage.c
+++ b/common/usb_storage.c
@@ -1209,6 +1209,18 @@
 			blkcnt -= blks;
 			break;
 		}
+		/*
+		 * A completed transfer proves the device is awake, so restore
+		 * the flag the error path above just cleared. Without this it
+		 * is cleared once and never set again for the rest of the boot:
+		 * usb_test_unit_ready() is the only other place that sets it,
+		 * and that runs at probe. Every command after the first error
+		 * then pays the 5ms wake-up delay in usb_stor_BBB_transport().
+		 * On an 84MB initrd at the stock 120KB transfer size that is
+		 * 714 chunks * 5ms = 3.6s, dropping throughput from 38MB/s to
+		 * 14.6MB/s for the rest of the boot.
+		 */
+		ss->flags |= USB_READY;
 		start += smallblks;
 		blks -= smallblks;
 		buf_addr += srb->datalen;
@@ -1296,6 +1308,8 @@
 			blkcnt -= blks;
 			break;
 		}
+		/* See usb_stor_read(): a completed transfer means it is awake. */
+		ss->flags |= USB_READY;
 		start += smallblks;
 		blks -= smallblks;
 		buf_addr += srb->datalen;
